(function(root){'use strict';root.LedgerEditorialManagementPairedA={"MA:B4":{"title":"Spreadsheets for management accounting models","sections":[["Build traceable management accounts","A spreadsheet is an accounting model, not evidence that the underlying inputs are correct. Organise original transaction data, assumptions, calculations and outputs separately, and label the period and unit of each column. Relative references move when copied; absolute references stay anchored to a chosen cell. A cost-rate cell used repeatedly in an overhead absorption calculation should be referenced consistently and protected against accidental overwriting."],["Use formulas to reconcile decisions","SUM, IF, lookup functions and simple arithmetic can automate a cash budget or variance report, but every formula must preserve the relevant cost and activity assumptions. Check grand totals against source data, investigate duplicate rows and specify whether cost data include tax. Protect model inputs, document versions and create simple integrity checks, such as opening cash plus receipts less payments equals closing cash. A printout should repeat headings and show units."],["Interpret spreadsheet errors as financial risks","A copied formula with an incorrectly moving standard-cost rate can produce a full column of plausible but wrong costs. Sorting only one column breaks the relationship between products, volumes and prices. A graph whose underlying rows include a total line can double-count output. Choose calculations and charts appropriate to the requested decision, and confirm that results remain correct after a changed assumption or new month is added."]],"workings":[["Fixed cell reference","A material standard cost is $4.20 per kg, stored in cell B2. Quantities of 300, 420 and 250 kg appear in C5:C7. Explain the formula pattern for calculating each row and the total standard cost.","In D5 enter =C5*$B$2 and fill down to D7, keeping the rate anchored. Individual costs are $1,260, $1,764 and $1,050; total =SUM(D5:D7) = $4,074. If B2 moved as the formula copied, the three products could receive inconsistent rates. Independently check 970 total kilograms × $4.20 = $4,074."],["Cash-budget integrity check","A cash budget opens with $8,000, has receipts of $27,500 and payments of $31,200. Its closing cash cell shows $6,400. Calculate the correct result and identify a useful check formula.","Closing cash should be $8,000 + $27,500 − $31,200 = $4,300, not $6,400. Create a check of closing minus opening minus receipts plus payments; it must return zero. Trace incorrect references, excluded payment rows, signs and periods rather than making an unexplained manual adjustment to the closing cell."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:B4":{"title":"Spreadsheets for management accounting models","sections":[["Build traceable management accounts","A spreadsheet is an accounting model, not evidence that the underlying inputs are correct. Organise original transaction data, assumptions, calculations and outputs separately, and label the period and unit of each column. Relative references move when copied; absolute references stay anchored to a chosen cell. A cost-rate cell used repeatedly in an overhead absorption calculation should be referenced consistently and protected against accidental overwriting."],["Use formulas to reconcile decisions","SUM, IF, lookup functions and simple arithmetic can automate a cash budget or variance report, but every formula must preserve the relevant cost and activity assumptions. Check grand totals against source data, investigate duplicate rows and specify whether cost data include tax. Protect model inputs, document versions and create simple integrity checks, such as opening cash plus receipts less payments equals closing cash. A printout should repeat headings and show units."],["Interpret spreadsheet errors as financial risks","A copied formula with an incorrectly moving standard-cost rate can produce a full column of plausible but wrong costs. Sorting only one column breaks the relationship between products, volumes and prices. A graph whose underlying rows include a total line can double-count output. Choose calculations and charts appropriate to the requested decision, and confirm that results remain correct after a changed assumption or new month is added."]],"workings":[["Fixed cell reference","A material standard cost is $4.20 per kg, stored in cell B2. Quantities of 300, 420 and 250 kg appear in C5:C7. Explain the formula pattern for calculating each row and the total standard cost.","In D5 enter =C5*$B$2 and fill down to D7, keeping the rate anchored. Individual costs are $1,260, $1,764 and $1,050; total =SUM(D5:D7) = $4,074. If B2 moved as the formula copied, the three products could receive inconsistent rates. Independently check 970 total kilograms × $4.20 = $4,074."],["Cash-budget integrity check","A cash budget opens with $8,000, has receipts of $27,500 and payments of $31,200. Its closing cash cell shows $6,400. Calculate the correct result and identify a useful check formula.","Closing cash should be $8,000 + $27,500 − $31,200 = $4,300, not $6,400. Create a check of closing minus opening minus receipts plus payments; it must return zero. Trace incorrect references, excluded payment rows, signs and periods rather than making an unexplained manual adjustment to the closing cell."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:B4"},"MA:C1":{"title":"Material, labour and overhead accounting","sections":[["Record material movement and inventory cost","Materials may be direct when traced economically to a cost object or indirect when supporting production generally. Record purchases, issues, returns, normal losses and inventory balances using consistent cost units. Relevant inventory methods such as FIFO or weighted average change the valuation of issues and closing inventory. Distinguish purchase price variance from physical usage differences; not all purchased inventory is consumed in the current output."],["Recognise labour and overhead correctly","Direct labour is attributable to a specific job or process; indirect labour and other factory support costs are overheads. Pay for idle time, overtime premiums and employer costs must be treated according to their economic cause and the specified costing convention. Accumulate overheads by cost centre, apportion shared costs on sensible bases, and absorb production overheads into output using the applicable rate. Actual overhead incurred and overhead absorbed rarely match exactly."],["Reconcile cost-flow outcomes","A production-cost report should link opening inventories, materials purchased and issued, labour costs, indirect costs and work in progress. A cost centre may have a high absorption rate because capacity is unused, not because each unit suddenly consumes more electricity. Show both the arithmetic and the causal interpretation before recommending an action. Common exam traps include using purchase quantity instead of material consumed or charging all payroll to direct labour."]],"workings":[["Direct and indirect manufacturing costs","A workshop uses direct materials $13,500, assembly wages $8,000, factory supervisors $2,400 and machinery electricity $1,100 during a period. Compute the simple prime cost and total factory production costs, assuming no inventory changes.","Prime cost = direct materials $13,500 + direct assembly labour $8,000 = $21,500. Factory overhead = supervisor $2,400 + machinery electricity $1,100 = $3,500. Total production cost = $25,000 before any inventory adjustment. The supervisor's work supports output collectively and cannot automatically be treated as direct labour."],["Overhead absorption variance","An entity budgets factory overhead $90,000 for 15,000 labour hours and incurs $91,000 while absorbing 14,500 hours. Compute the absorption rate and the total under- or over-absorption.","Predetermined rate = $90,000 / 15,000 = $6 per labour hour. Absorbed overhead = 14,500 × $6 = $87,000. Actual cost $91,000 exceeds absorbed overhead by $4,000, so overhead is under-absorbed by $4,000. Explain separately whether spending, activity or efficiency drove the difference."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:C1":{"title":"Material, labour and overhead accounting","sections":[["Record material movement and inventory cost","Materials may be direct when traced economically to a cost object or indirect when supporting production generally. Record purchases, issues, returns, normal losses and inventory balances using consistent cost units. Relevant inventory methods such as FIFO or weighted average change the valuation of issues and closing inventory. Distinguish purchase price variance from physical usage differences; not all purchased inventory is consumed in the current output."],["Recognise labour and overhead correctly","Direct labour is attributable to a specific job or process; indirect labour and other factory support costs are overheads. Pay for idle time, overtime premiums and employer costs must be treated according to their economic cause and the specified costing convention. Accumulate overheads by cost centre, apportion shared costs on sensible bases, and absorb production overheads into output using the applicable rate. Actual overhead incurred and overhead absorbed rarely match exactly."],["Reconcile cost-flow outcomes","A production-cost report should link opening inventories, materials purchased and issued, labour costs, indirect costs and work in progress. A cost centre may have a high absorption rate because capacity is unused, not because each unit suddenly consumes more electricity. Show both the arithmetic and the causal interpretation before recommending an action. Common exam traps include using purchase quantity instead of material consumed or charging all payroll to direct labour."]],"workings":[["Direct and indirect manufacturing costs","A workshop uses direct materials $13,500, assembly wages $8,000, factory supervisors $2,400 and machinery electricity $1,100 during a period. Compute the simple prime cost and total factory production costs, assuming no inventory changes.","Prime cost = direct materials $13,500 + direct assembly labour $8,000 = $21,500. Factory overhead = supervisor $2,400 + machinery electricity $1,100 = $3,500. Total production cost = $25,000 before any inventory adjustment. The supervisor's work supports output collectively and cannot automatically be treated as direct labour."],["Overhead absorption variance","An entity budgets factory overhead $90,000 for 15,000 labour hours and incurs $91,000 while absorbing 14,500 hours. Compute the absorption rate and the total under- or over-absorption.","Predetermined rate = $90,000 / 15,000 = $6 per labour hour. Absorbed overhead = 14,500 × $6 = $87,000. Actual cost $91,000 exceeds absorbed overhead by $4,000, so overhead is under-absorbed by $4,000. Explain separately whether spending, activity or efficiency drove the difference."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:C1"},"MA:C3":{"title":"Job, batch, process and service costing","sections":[["Select a costing method based on production","Job costing accumulates costs for a distinct customer order, batch costing for a group of similar units, and process costing for continuous or homogeneous output. Service costing often uses a composite unit such as passenger-kilometres or patient-days. The method must fit the way resources are consumed; a process manufacturer cannot measure cost sensibly by pretending that every litre is a separate commissioned job."],["Prepare cost cards and flow reconciliations","A job cost card collects direct materials, direct labour and absorbed overhead before comparing the result with quoted price. Batch cost is divided by accepted units after considering relevant wastage assumptions. Process accounting reconciles input units with good output, normal losses and abnormal losses and may use equivalent units to value incomplete work. For a service, define the unit consistently and ensure numerator and denominator refer to the same activity period."],["Use results cautiously","A large average process cost can mask a bottleneck or an abnormal loss. A low cost per passenger-kilometre may result from fuller buses rather than lower fuel cost per journey. For quotations distinguish total cost, mark-up and margin, and check whether allocated fixed overhead is relevant to an incremental decision. Select the appropriate activity measure before applying the reported cost to a new situation."]],"workings":[["Job cost and selling price","A custom order consumes $1,800 materials and 60 direct labour hours at $18/hour. Overheads are absorbed at $12 per labour hour. Calculate total job cost and a selling price using a 25% mark-up on cost.","Direct labour = 60 × $18 = $1,080; absorbed overhead = 60 × $12 = $720. Total job cost = $1,800 + $1,080 + $720 = $3,600. At a 25% mark-up, proposed selling price = $3,600 × 1.25 = $4,500. A 25% profit margin on selling price would require a different computation."],["Batch costing with rejects","A batch costs $14,400 in total; 600 units enter production, of which 40 fail final inspection and have no recoverable value. What is cost per acceptable unit, assuming all costs are absorbed by accepted output?","Acceptable units = 600 − 40 = 560. Average cost per accepted unit = $14,400 / 560 ≈ $25.71. Dividing by all 600 units ($24) understates the cost of saleable output under these assumptions. Investigate whether the rejects are normal or abnormal before applying the relevant full process-costing treatment."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:C3":{"title":"Job, batch, process and service costing","sections":[["Select a costing method based on production","Job costing accumulates costs for a distinct customer order, batch costing for a group of similar units, and process costing for continuous or homogeneous output. Service costing often uses a composite unit such as passenger-kilometres or patient-days. The method must fit the way resources are consumed; a process manufacturer cannot measure cost sensibly by pretending that every litre is a separate commissioned job."],["Prepare cost cards and flow reconciliations","A job cost card collects direct materials, direct labour and absorbed overhead before comparing the result with quoted price. Batch cost is divided by accepted units after considering relevant wastage assumptions. Process accounting reconciles input units with good output, normal losses and abnormal losses and may use equivalent units to value incomplete work. For a service, define the unit consistently and ensure numerator and denominator refer to the same activity period."],["Use results cautiously","A large average process cost can mask a bottleneck or an abnormal loss. A low cost per passenger-kilometre may result from fuller buses rather than lower fuel cost per journey. For quotations distinguish total cost, mark-up and margin, and check whether allocated fixed overhead is relevant to an incremental decision. Select the appropriate activity measure before applying the reported cost to a new situation."]],"workings":[["Job cost and selling price","A custom order consumes $1,800 materials and 60 direct labour hours at $18/hour. Overheads are absorbed at $12 per labour hour. Calculate total job cost and a selling price using a 25% mark-up on cost.","Direct labour = 60 × $18 = $1,080; absorbed overhead = 60 × $12 = $720. Total job cost = $1,800 + $1,080 + $720 = $3,600. At a 25% mark-up, proposed selling price = $3,600 × 1.25 = $4,500. A 25% profit margin on selling price would require a different computation."],["Batch costing with rejects","A batch costs $14,400 in total; 600 units enter production, of which 40 fail final inspection and have no recoverable value. What is cost per acceptable unit, assuming all costs are absorbed by accepted output?","Acceptable units = 600 − 40 = 560. Average cost per accepted unit = $14,400 / 560 ≈ $25.71. Dividing by all 600 units ($24) understates the cost of saleable output under these assumptions. Investigate whether the rejects are normal or abnormal before applying the relevant full process-costing treatment."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:C3"},"MA:C4":{"title":"Alternative costing principles and relevant decisions","sections":[["Differentiate full and marginal cost","Absorption costing charges production fixed overhead to units alongside variable production cost; marginal costing treats fixed production overhead as a period cost and focuses on contribution. Profits may differ when inventory volumes change because absorption costing carries part of fixed production overhead in closing inventory. A contribution calculation is valuable for short-run decisions but is not a substitute for the full costing and financial reporting rules."],["Apply activity-based insights","Activity-based costing attributes support costs through activities and cost drivers such as setups, inspections or orders rather than allocating everything by labour hours. It can reveal high-complexity products consuming disproportionate support resources. But driver collection costs money and a plausible driver is not always causal. Compare ABC and traditional costing on the same product mix and calculate whether a changed product price or design becomes justifiable."],["Select a decision-relevant basis","Relevant cost is a future incremental cash flow that changes between alternatives; sunk historical cost and unchanged fixed allocations are usually irrelevant. Opportunity cost may matter even when no new invoice is issued. Avoid using a full absorption cost as the only 'make or buy' decision input or confusing variable cost per unit with total variable cost. Explain capacity, quality, supplier reliability and strategic consequences alongside arithmetic."]],"workings":[["Marginal versus absorption difference","A company produces 10,000 units and sells 8,000. Fixed production overhead is $50,000 and the predetermined overhead rate is $5 per unit; there is no opening inventory or variances. Explain the profit difference.","Absorption closing inventory includes 2,000 × $5 = $10,000 fixed production overhead deferred to a future period. Absorption profit therefore exceeds marginal-costing profit by $10,000 on these simplified facts. Under marginal costing all $50,000 fixed production overhead is charged in the period. Verify the direction using the closing inventory increase."],["ABC versus one volume driver","A product uses 20 machine hours but requires five separate setups. The factory's setup pool is $30,000 for 60 setups. Compute setup cost attributed under ABC and explain why labour-hours-only allocation can mislead.","Setup-driver rate = $30,000 / 60 = $500 per setup. The product receives 5 × $500 = $2,500 of setup activity cost. A labour-hour allocation might obscure this batch complexity, particularly if setups are unrelated to labour hours. ABC improves insight only when the setup count is a relevant causal driver and the total cost pool has been defined properly."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:C4":{"title":"Alternative costing principles and relevant decisions","sections":[["Differentiate full and marginal cost","Absorption costing charges production fixed overhead to units alongside variable production cost; marginal costing treats fixed production overhead as a period cost and focuses on contribution. Profits may differ when inventory volumes change because absorption costing carries part of fixed production overhead in closing inventory. A contribution calculation is valuable for short-run decisions but is not a substitute for the full costing and financial reporting rules."],["Apply activity-based insights","Activity-based costing attributes support costs through activities and cost drivers such as setups, inspections or orders rather than allocating everything by labour hours. It can reveal high-complexity products consuming disproportionate support resources. But driver collection costs money and a plausible driver is not always causal. Compare ABC and traditional costing on the same product mix and calculate whether a changed product price or design becomes justifiable."],["Select a decision-relevant basis","Relevant cost is a future incremental cash flow that changes between alternatives; sunk historical cost and unchanged fixed allocations are usually irrelevant. Opportunity cost may matter even when no new invoice is issued. Avoid using a full absorption cost as the only 'make or buy' decision input or confusing variable cost per unit with total variable cost. Explain capacity, quality, supplier reliability and strategic consequences alongside arithmetic."]],"workings":[["Marginal versus absorption difference","A company produces 10,000 units and sells 8,000. Fixed production overhead is $50,000 and the predetermined overhead rate is $5 per unit; there is no opening inventory or variances. Explain the profit difference.","Absorption closing inventory includes 2,000 × $5 = $10,000 fixed production overhead deferred to a future period. Absorption profit therefore exceeds marginal-costing profit by $10,000 on these simplified facts. Under marginal costing all $50,000 fixed production overhead is charged in the period. Verify the direction using the closing inventory increase."],["ABC versus one volume driver","A product uses 20 machine hours but requires five separate setups. The factory's setup pool is $30,000 for 60 setups. Compute setup cost attributed under ABC and explain why labour-hours-only allocation can mislead.","Setup-driver rate = $30,000 / 60 = $500 per setup. The product receives 5 × $500 = $2,500 of setup activity cost. A labour-hour allocation might obscure this batch complexity, particularly if setups are unrelated to labour hours. ABC improves insight only when the setup count is a relevant causal driver and the total cost pool has been defined properly."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:C4"},"MA:D1":{"title":"Budgeting objectives, coordination and behavioural implications","sections":[["Budget as plan, benchmark and communication","A budget translates organisational objectives into quantified operations and resource commitments. It coordinates demand, production, staffing, purchasing, financing and cash. The initial sales budget may constrain material and production plans, while capacity or funding can also be the limiting factor. A budget is not a historical fact and must distinguish forecast assumptions from management-approved targets."],["Compare approaches and purposes","Incremental budgets start from prior allocations, zero-based budgets require justified activities, and rolling budgets extend the horizon as periods pass. Fixed budgets hold activity assumptions constant; flexed budgets adjust suitable variable elements to actual activity for fairer performance comparison. Participation may increase ownership but can encourage budgetary slack. The right design depends on uncertainty, decision needs and available cost-driver information."],["Avoid performance distortions","An overly demanding target can encourage staff to defer necessary maintenance or report sales early. A favourable spending variance is not automatically evidence of good management if output falls or quality deteriorates. Set responsibility for controllable items, document assumptions and explain variances along with customer, process and safety indicators. Use budgets as structured evidence for management action, not as proof that every deviation is an employee failure."]],"workings":[["Budget functions","A hospital budgets ward staffing, drug inventory and monthly cash and then uses the original budget to punish managers despite a 20% rise in patient volume. Identify two coordination benefits and the principal control flaw.","The staffing and drug budgets coordinate procurement, service capacity and funding, while the cash budget highlights when payments must be financed. Comparing actual costs with an unchanged budget despite 20% higher activity can be unfair. Flex activity-sensitive allowances and investigate unit-cost efficiency, quality and access before attributing any adverse amount to managerial failure."],["Participative budget slack","A department's realistic annual materials requirement is $180,000, but its manager asks for $210,000 to ensure an easy favourable variance. Compute the built-in buffer and explain the governance risk.","The requested allowance exceeds the evidence-based forecast by $30,000, equivalent to about 16.7% of the realistic cost estimate. This can misallocate scarce funds and weaken performance information. Require documented volume and rate assumptions, peer challenge and realistic contingency planning rather than arbitrarily eliminating all uncertainty."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:D1":{"title":"Budgeting objectives, coordination and behavioural implications","sections":[["Budget as plan, benchmark and communication","A budget translates organisational objectives into quantified operations and resource commitments. It coordinates demand, production, staffing, purchasing, financing and cash. The initial sales budget may constrain material and production plans, while capacity or funding can also be the limiting factor. A budget is not a historical fact and must distinguish forecast assumptions from management-approved targets."],["Compare approaches and purposes","Incremental budgets start from prior allocations, zero-based budgets require justified activities, and rolling budgets extend the horizon as periods pass. Fixed budgets hold activity assumptions constant; flexed budgets adjust suitable variable elements to actual activity for fairer performance comparison. Participation may increase ownership but can encourage budgetary slack. The right design depends on uncertainty, decision needs and available cost-driver information."],["Avoid performance distortions","An overly demanding target can encourage staff to defer necessary maintenance or report sales early. A favourable spending variance is not automatically evidence of good management if output falls or quality deteriorates. Set responsibility for controllable items, document assumptions and explain variances along with customer, process and safety indicators. Use budgets as structured evidence for management action, not as proof that every deviation is an employee failure."]],"workings":[["Budget functions","A hospital budgets ward staffing, drug inventory and monthly cash and then uses the original budget to punish managers despite a 20% rise in patient volume. Identify two coordination benefits and the principal control flaw.","The staffing and drug budgets coordinate procurement, service capacity and funding, while the cash budget highlights when payments must be financed. Comparing actual costs with an unchanged budget despite 20% higher activity can be unfair. Flex activity-sensitive allowances and investigate unit-cost efficiency, quality and access before attributing any adverse amount to managerial failure."],["Participative budget slack","A department's realistic annual materials requirement is $180,000, but its manager asks for $210,000 to ensure an easy favourable variance. Compute the built-in buffer and explain the governance risk.","The requested allowance exceeds the evidence-based forecast by $30,000, equivalent to about 16.7% of the realistic cost estimate. This can misallocate scarce funds and weaken performance information. Require documented volume and rate assumptions, peer challenge and realistic contingency planning rather than arbitrarily eliminating all uncertainty."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:D1"},"MA:D2":{"title":"Build coherent operational and financial budgets","sections":[["Sequence subsidiary budgets logically","A comprehensive budget translates demand into production, purchases, staffing, overhead and financial projections. A common production formula is budgeted sales units + desired closing finished-goods units − opening finished-goods units. Direct materials purchases follow production requirements plus desired closing material inventory less opening material inventory. Financial statements and cash budgets must be consistent with these operational quantities and timing assumptions."],["Separate accrual costs from cash movement","Purchases affect payables, while cash payments depend on supplier credit terms. Sales may be budgeted in one month but collected later, so a profit forecast cannot be substituted for a cash budget. Capital expenditure, loan proceeds, interest and drawings must be classified appropriately. Reconcile cash opening plus receipts less payments and check closing inventory quantities against the next period's production assumptions."],["Identify limiting factors and sensitivity","Capacity, skilled hours, machine time, materials availability or finance may constrain the sales-led plan. Document assumptions on scrap, yield, overtime and prices. Scenario analysis tests a plausible range of demand and cost drivers rather than promising one precise annual total. A mathematically reconciled budget can still be operationally impossible; confirm capacity and working-capital feasibility before recommending approval."]],"workings":[["Production and material purchases","Budgeted sales are 7,000 units, opening finished inventory 600 and desired closing inventory 900. Each unit needs 3 kg of material; material inventory rises from 1,200 to 1,500 kg. Compute planned production and material purchases.","Production = 7,000 + 900 − 600 = 7,300 finished units. Material required for production = 7,300 × 3 = 21,900 kg. Purchases = 21,900 + 1,500 desired closing − 1,200 opening = 22,200 kg. Do not confuse material usage with purchases or double-count finished-goods opening stock."],["Receipts timing","A business budgets $50,000 credit sales in May and expects 70% of sales to be collected in the following month, with the remaining 30% two months later. Ignoring defaults, how much May sales cash arrives in June and July?","May sales generate $35,000 cash in June (70% × $50,000) and $15,000 cash in July (30% × $50,000), not $50,000 in May. A complete cash budget must also include receipts from earlier sales and any current-month cash sales. The timing is essential even if reported revenue is correctly accrued in May."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:D2":{"title":"Build coherent operational and financial budgets","sections":[["Sequence subsidiary budgets logically","A comprehensive budget translates demand into production, purchases, staffing, overhead and financial projections. A common production formula is budgeted sales units + desired closing finished-goods units − opening finished-goods units. Direct materials purchases follow production requirements plus desired closing material inventory less opening material inventory. Financial statements and cash budgets must be consistent with these operational quantities and timing assumptions."],["Separate accrual costs from cash movement","Purchases affect payables, while cash payments depend on supplier credit terms. Sales may be budgeted in one month but collected later, so a profit forecast cannot be substituted for a cash budget. Capital expenditure, loan proceeds, interest and drawings must be classified appropriately. Reconcile cash opening plus receipts less payments and check closing inventory quantities against the next period's production assumptions."],["Identify limiting factors and sensitivity","Capacity, skilled hours, machine time, materials availability or finance may constrain the sales-led plan. Document assumptions on scrap, yield, overtime and prices. Scenario analysis tests a plausible range of demand and cost drivers rather than promising one precise annual total. A mathematically reconciled budget can still be operationally impossible; confirm capacity and working-capital feasibility before recommending approval."]],"workings":[["Production and material purchases","Budgeted sales are 7,000 units, opening finished inventory 600 and desired closing inventory 900. Each unit needs 3 kg of material; material inventory rises from 1,200 to 1,500 kg. Compute planned production and material purchases.","Production = 7,000 + 900 − 600 = 7,300 finished units. Material required for production = 7,300 × 3 = 21,900 kg. Purchases = 21,900 + 1,500 desired closing − 1,200 opening = 22,200 kg. Do not confuse material usage with purchases or double-count finished-goods opening stock."],["Receipts timing","A business budgets $50,000 credit sales in May and expects 70% of sales to be collected in the following month, with the remaining 30% two months later. Ignoring defaults, how much May sales cash arrives in June and July?","May sales generate $35,000 cash in June (70% × $50,000) and $15,000 cash in July (30% × $50,000), not $50,000 in May. A complete cash budget must also include receipts from earlier sales and any current-month cash sales. The timing is essential even if reported revenue is correctly accrued in May."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:D2"},"MA:D3":{"title":"Flexible budgets and activity-appropriate cost control","sections":[["Distinguish fixed budget and flexed allowance","A fixed original budget reflects one planned activity level. Flexing restates the variable-cost allowance for actual output or hours while keeping genuinely fixed costs stable within the relevant range. Mixed costs require separating fixed and variable components. Flexing supports like-for-like comparison but does not excuse inefficiency: actual resource consumption may still exceed the amount allowed for the actual activity."],["Calculate and classify differences","State the actual activity and per-unit allowance before computing flexed variable cost. A cost variance is favourable when actual cost is below the comparable flexed allowance, unless the measure or business context changes the interpretation. Reconcile to the fixed-budget difference by separating volume effects from spending or efficiency effects. Do not scale fixed overhead automatically when output rises."],["Question underlying cost behaviour","The relationship between cost and activity may be nonlinear, step-fixed or changed by learning curves and shortages. A flexed budget based on old rates can misstate what should be achievable in current conditions. Investigate supplier prices, overtime, yield and quality before assigning responsibility. In an exam, label all calculations with units and distinguish variances caused by greater production from those caused by cost control."]],"workings":[["Flex mixed-cost budget","The original plan covers 5,000 units with $10 variable cost per unit and fixed costs of $32,000. Actual output is 5,800 and actual total cost $91,000. Calculate flexed total cost and the variance.","Flexed variable cost = 5,800 × $10 = $58,000; fixed allowance stays $32,000. Flexed total = $90,000. Actual total cost $91,000 is $1,000 adverse relative to actual activity. The original total budget $82,000 would exaggerate the apparent adverse amount by treating the extra 800 units as if they used no resources."],["Step cost versus fixed assumption","A workshop's supervisor cost is $12,000 per month for up to 4,000 units but requires an additional $3,000 when output exceeds 4,000. Actual production is 4,300 units. Explain the fixed-budget adjustment.","At 4,300 units the relevant supervisor allowance is $15,000, not an automatically proportional $12,900 or a constant $12,000. The cost is step-fixed within specified capacity bands. Add the $3,000 when flexing the budget for this output level, then compare actual staffing cost with $15,000."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:D3":{"title":"Flexible budgets and activity-appropriate cost control","sections":[["Distinguish fixed budget and flexed allowance","A fixed original budget reflects one planned activity level. Flexing restates the variable-cost allowance for actual output or hours while keeping genuinely fixed costs stable within the relevant range. Mixed costs require separating fixed and variable components. Flexing supports like-for-like comparison but does not excuse inefficiency: actual resource consumption may still exceed the amount allowed for the actual activity."],["Calculate and classify differences","State the actual activity and per-unit allowance before computing flexed variable cost. A cost variance is favourable when actual cost is below the comparable flexed allowance, unless the measure or business context changes the interpretation. Reconcile to the fixed-budget difference by separating volume effects from spending or efficiency effects. Do not scale fixed overhead automatically when output rises."],["Question underlying cost behaviour","The relationship between cost and activity may be nonlinear, step-fixed or changed by learning curves and shortages. A flexed budget based on old rates can misstate what should be achievable in current conditions. Investigate supplier prices, overtime, yield and quality before assigning responsibility. In an exam, label all calculations with units and distinguish variances caused by greater production from those caused by cost control."]],"workings":[["Flex mixed-cost budget","The original plan covers 5,000 units with $10 variable cost per unit and fixed costs of $32,000. Actual output is 5,800 and actual total cost $91,000. Calculate flexed total cost and the variance.","Flexed variable cost = 5,800 × $10 = $58,000; fixed allowance stays $32,000. Flexed total = $90,000. Actual total cost $91,000 is $1,000 adverse relative to actual activity. The original total budget $82,000 would exaggerate the apparent adverse amount by treating the extra 800 units as if they used no resources."],["Step cost versus fixed assumption","A workshop's supervisor cost is $12,000 per month for up to 4,000 units but requires an additional $3,000 when output exceeds 4,000. Actual production is 4,300 units. Explain the fixed-budget adjustment.","At 4,300 units the relevant supervisor allowance is $15,000, not an automatically proportional $12,900 or a constant $12,000. The cost is step-fixed within specified capacity bands. Add the $3,000 when flexing the budget for this output level, then compare actual staffing cost with $15,000."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:D3"},"MA:D4":{"title":"Capital budgeting, payback and discounted cash flows","sections":[["Define relevant project cash flows","Capital investment appraisal compares incremental future cash outflows with economic benefits. Identify initial investment, project cash flows, working-capital commitments, disposal proceeds and relevant taxes where examinable. Ignore sunk costs already incurred. Payback reports the recovery period without properly valuing all subsequent cash flows; net present value discounts timing and reflects the required return under consistent assumptions."],["Compute NPV carefully","Discount each end-of-year cash flow using its period-specific discount factor and subtract initial outlay. A positive NPV indicates project value relative to the specified discount rate if forecast cash flows and other assumptions hold. Internal rate of return is the rate making NPV zero and can be misleading for mutually exclusive projects or non-conventional cash-flow patterns. Use nominal cash flows with nominal rates and real cash flows with real rates."],["Evaluate constraints and qualitative effects","Risk, financing liquidity, capacity, environmental impact and strategic fit can alter a decision. A short payback period does not ensure profitability, and a positive NPV is not a guarantee of realised value. Test important sensitivities and distinguish profitability from cash availability. Exam answers should show a discounting schedule, units, final recommendation and limits of the model; rounded discount factors can cause small differences."]],"workings":[["Two-year investment NPV","A project costs $100,000 now and generates $60,000 at each of the ends of years one and two. Required return is 10%. Calculate NPV and state the accept/reject criterion.","Present values are $60,000/1.10 = $54,545.45 and $60,000/1.10² = $49,586.78. Total PV = $104,132.23; NPV ≈ $4,132.23 positive. On these assumptions the project adds value relative to a 10% required return. It must still be assessed against mutually exclusive alternatives, liquidity constraints and forecast risk."],["Payback versus final-year cash","Two projects each cost $50,000. Project A returns $25,000 in each of years 1 and 2 then zero; project B returns $20,000 in years 1 and 2 and $30,000 in year 3. Explain why simple payback alone may choose incorrectly.","A has payback 2 years; B has payback approximately 2 + ($10,000/$30,000) = 2.33 years. But B returns $70,000 in total while A returns $50,000, and discounting may favour B depending on the required rate. Payback ignores the full project cash profile and time value of money; compute NPV before treating A as economically superior."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:D4":{"title":"Capital budgeting, payback and discounted cash flows","sections":[["Define relevant project cash flows","Capital investment appraisal compares incremental future cash outflows with economic benefits. Identify initial investment, project cash flows, working-capital commitments, disposal proceeds and relevant taxes where examinable. Ignore sunk costs already incurred. Payback reports the recovery period without properly valuing all subsequent cash flows; net present value discounts timing and reflects the required return under consistent assumptions."],["Compute NPV carefully","Discount each end-of-year cash flow using its period-specific discount factor and subtract initial outlay. A positive NPV indicates project value relative to the specified discount rate if forecast cash flows and other assumptions hold. Internal rate of return is the rate making NPV zero and can be misleading for mutually exclusive projects or non-conventional cash-flow patterns. Use nominal cash flows with nominal rates and real cash flows with real rates."],["Evaluate constraints and qualitative effects","Risk, financing liquidity, capacity, environmental impact and strategic fit can alter a decision. A short payback period does not ensure profitability, and a positive NPV is not a guarantee of realised value. Test important sensitivities and distinguish profitability from cash availability. Exam answers should show a discounting schedule, units, final recommendation and limits of the model; rounded discount factors can cause small differences."]],"workings":[["Two-year investment NPV","A project costs $100,000 now and generates $60,000 at each of the ends of years one and two. Required return is 10%. Calculate NPV and state the accept/reject criterion.","Present values are $60,000/1.10 = $54,545.45 and $60,000/1.10² = $49,586.78. Total PV = $104,132.23; NPV ≈ $4,132.23 positive. On these assumptions the project adds value relative to a 10% required return. It must still be assessed against mutually exclusive alternatives, liquidity constraints and forecast risk."],["Payback versus final-year cash","Two projects each cost $50,000. Project A returns $25,000 in each of years 1 and 2 then zero; project B returns $20,000 in years 1 and 2 and $30,000 in year 3. Explain why simple payback alone may choose incorrectly.","A has payback 2 years; B has payback approximately 2 + ($10,000/$30,000) = 2.33 years. But B returns $70,000 in total while A returns $50,000, and discounting may favour B depending on the required rate. Payback ignores the full project cash profile and time value of money; compute NPV before treating A as economically superior."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:D4"},"MA:D5":{"title":"Budgetary control, responsibility and variance reporting","sections":[["Turn a comparison into a control process","Budgetary control compares actual outcomes with suitable standards, explains exceptions and guides corrective decisions. Apply responsibility accounting by distinguishing costs a manager can influence from uncontrollable central charges. An exception report should show actual, flexed or fixed budget as appropriate, variance, explanation, confidence in the data and action owner. Merely generating a red or green number is not a managerial diagnosis."],["Interpret causes and related indicators","An adverse material-cost variance may reflect price, quantity, substitution, supplier quality or inaccurate standards. A favourable labour-cost variance might be driven by fewer hours but also training cuts or output defects. Where actual volume differs from plan, avoid judging efficiency against the wrong activity level. Consider whether original assumptions were valid and whether significant changes warrant a revised forecast without rewriting performance history."],["Document action and avoid perverse incentives","Investigate material deviations promptly and prioritise by financial significance, recurrence and operational risk. Recommendations should distinguish immediate containment, root-cause analysis and long-term improvement. A budget manager may intentionally accept higher spending for safety, product quality or resilience, so conclusions must address non-financial trade-offs. Follow up to determine whether corrective action actually improved outcomes."]],"workings":[["Budget versus comparable activity","Budgeted variable materials cost is $6 per unit for 10,000 units, with fixed factory cost $30,000. Actual output is 11,000 units, and actual combined cost is $99,000. Calculate comparable budget and interpret.","Flexed material allowance = 11,000 × $6 = $66,000; fixed cost $30,000; flexed total = $96,000. Actual $99,000 is $3,000 adverse. Comparing actual cost only with the original $90,000 would produce $9,000 adverse, but $6,000 of that reflects output growth at the planned variable rate. Investigate the remaining $3,000."],["Variance management decision","A purchasing team achieves a $7,000 favourable price variance by buying cheaper components, but rework cost rises $12,000 and defect rates increase. Is its sourcing change cost-effective?","If the extra $12,000 rework cost is attributable to the lower-quality inputs, the apparent $7,000 saving is exceeded by $5,000 direct rework cost before considering customer complaints, warranties and reputational damage. Investigate causal evidence, yields, supplier specifications and any price-quality trade-off. Do not reward a local favourable variance without assessing total economic impact."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"MA ACCA Applied Knowledge 2026–27","qualificationContext":"ACCA Applied Knowledge"},"FMA:D5":{"title":"Budgetary control, responsibility and variance reporting","sections":[["Turn a comparison into a control process","Budgetary control compares actual outcomes with suitable standards, explains exceptions and guides corrective decisions. Apply responsibility accounting by distinguishing costs a manager can influence from uncontrollable central charges. An exception report should show actual, flexed or fixed budget as appropriate, variance, explanation, confidence in the data and action owner. Merely generating a red or green number is not a managerial diagnosis."],["Interpret causes and related indicators","An adverse material-cost variance may reflect price, quantity, substitution, supplier quality or inaccurate standards. A favourable labour-cost variance might be driven by fewer hours but also training cuts or output defects. Where actual volume differs from plan, avoid judging efficiency against the wrong activity level. Consider whether original assumptions were valid and whether significant changes warrant a revised forecast without rewriting performance history."],["Document action and avoid perverse incentives","Investigate material deviations promptly and prioritise by financial significance, recurrence and operational risk. Recommendations should distinguish immediate containment, root-cause analysis and long-term improvement. A budget manager may intentionally accept higher spending for safety, product quality or resilience, so conclusions must address non-financial trade-offs. Follow up to determine whether corrective action actually improved outcomes."]],"workings":[["Budget versus comparable activity","Budgeted variable materials cost is $6 per unit for 10,000 units, with fixed factory cost $30,000. Actual output is 11,000 units, and actual combined cost is $99,000. Calculate comparable budget and interpret.","Flexed material allowance = 11,000 × $6 = $66,000; fixed cost $30,000; flexed total = $96,000. Actual $99,000 is $3,000 adverse. Comparing actual cost only with the original $90,000 would produce $9,000 adverse, but $6,000 of that reflects output growth at the planned variable rate. Investigate the remaining $3,000."],["Variance management decision","A purchasing team achieves a $7,000 favourable price variance by buying cheaper components, but rework cost rises $12,000 and defect rates increase. Is its sourcing change cost-effective?","If the extra $12,000 rework cost is attributable to the lower-quality inputs, the apparent $7,000 saving is exceeded by $5,000 direct rework cost before considering customer complaints, warranties and reputational damage. Investigate causal evidence, yields, supplier specifications and any price-quality trade-off. Do not reward a local favourable variance without assessing total economic impact."]],"sourceKind":"paired-syllabus-original","reviewStatus":"DETAILED-OUTCOME REVIEW PENDING","edition":"FMA FIA 2026–27","qualificationContext":"FIA Foundations in Accountancy","adaptedFrom":"MA:D5"}};})(typeof window!=='undefined'?window:globalThis);
